from science_completion import q,p,m
QUESTIONS=[
q(1,'Convert 250 mA to amperes.','0.250 A.','MS1a;WS4.5'),
q(1,'Convert 3.0 minutes to seconds.','180 s.','MS1a;WS4.5'),
q(1,'Write 72,000 in standard form.','7.2 × 10⁴.','MS1b'),
q(1,'How many significant figures are in 0.00620?','Three.','MS2a'),
q(1,'What unit is obtained by dividing energy in joules by time in seconds?','Watt (W).','MS3c;Appendix A'),
q(1,'What does the symbol ∝ mean?','Proportional to.','MS3a'),
q(1,'What quantity is given by the gradient of a distance–time graph?','Speed.','MS4d–4e'),
q(1,'What quantity is given by the signed area under a velocity–time graph?','Displacement.','MS4f'),
q(2,'Convert 8.0 cm² to m².','1 cm² = 10⁻⁴ m², so **8.0 cm² = 8.0 × 10⁻⁴ m²**.','MS1a;WS4.5'),
q(2,'Convert 25 cm³ to m³.','1 cm³ = 10⁻⁶ m³, so **25 cm³ = 2.5 × 10⁻⁵ m³**.','MS1b;WS4.5'),
q(2,'A potential difference of 9.0 V causes a current of 0.30 A. Calculate resistance.','**R = V/I = 9.0/0.30 = 30 Ω**.','MS3b–3c'),
q(2,'A speed is 72 km/h. Convert it to m/s.','72/3.6 = **20 m/s**.','MS1a;WS4.5'),
q(2,'A resistor’s I–V graph has current on y and voltage on x. What does its straight-line gradient equal?','- Gradient = I/V = **1/R**.\n- Reversing the axes to V against I would give **R**.','MS4d'),
q(2,'Explain why a straight-line graph with a non-zero y-intercept does not show direct proportionality between y and x.','- Direct proportion has **y = kx**.\n- It requires a line **through the origin**; a non-zero intercept adds a constant term.','MS3a;MS4b'),
q(2,'A power output rises from 80 W to 100 W. Calculate the percentage increase.','Percentage increase = (100 − 80)/80 × 100 = **25%**.','MS1c'),
q(2,'At fixed mass, what happens to kinetic energy if speed triples? Explain using the relationship.','- **Eₖ ∝ v²**.\n- Tripling speed multiplies kinetic energy by **3² = 9**.','MS1c;MS3a'),
q(2,'An echo returns after 0.10 s in water where wave speed is 1,500 m/s. Calculate one-way distance.','**Distance = vt/2 = 1,500 × 0.10/2 = 75 m**.','MS3c;4.6.1.5'),
q(2,'A 0.050 m length has estimated uncertainty ±0.001 m. Calculate percentage uncertainty.','0.001/0.050 × 100 = **2%**.','MS1c;WS3.4'),
q(2,'A histogram bar covers a width of 5 units and represents 20 readings. Calculate its frequency density.','Frequency density = frequency/interval width = 20/5 = **4 readings per unit**.','MS2c'),
q(2,'Round 0.004867 to three significant figures.','**0.00487**. Leading zeros are not significant; retain 4, 8 and rounded 7.','MS2a'),
q(3,'Calculate speed from kinetic energy 36 J and mass 2.0 kg.','- Eₖ = ½mv² gives **v = √(2Eₖ/m)**.\n- v = √(2 × 36/2.0) = √36.\n- **v = 6.0 m/s**.','MS3b–3d'),
q(3,'A gas at 100 kPa occupies 60 cm³. It is compressed to 40 cm³ without changing temperature or gas mass. Calculate final pressure.','- **p₁V₁ = p₂V₂**.\n- p₂ = 100 × 60/40.\n- **p₂ = 150 kPa**. Matching volume units are used on both sides.','MS3b–3c'),
q(3,'A force–extension best-fit line passes through (0.020 m, 4 N) and (0.050 m, 10 N). Calculate gradient and identify its physical meaning.','- Gradient = (10 − 4)/(0.050 − 0.020).\n- **200 N/m**.\n- It is the **spring constant k** in the proportional region.','MS4d;4.5.3'),
q(3,'A tangent to a distance–time graph rises by 18 m over 3.0 s. Calculate the speed and explain why the tangent is used.','- Gradient = 18/3.0 = **6.0 m/s**.\n- A tangent gives the rate of change **at the chosen point**.\n- It estimates **instantaneous speed**, unlike a chord’s interval-average rate.','MS4e'),
q(3,'Find the mean, median and mode of 20, 21, 21, 22 and 26.','- Mean = 110/5 = **22**.\n- Median = middle sorted value = **21**.\n- Mode = most frequent value = **21**.','MS2b;MS2f'),
q(3,'A frequency table records value 2 three times, value 3 four times and value 5 once. Calculate the mean.','- Weighted sum = 2 × 3 + 3 × 4 + 5 × 1 = **23**.\n- Total frequency = 3 + 4 + 1 = **8**.\n- Mean = 23/8 = **2.875**, about 2.9 at two significant figures.','MS2b–2c'),
q(3,'A cube has side length 3.0 cm. Calculate its volume in cm³ and m³, and its total surface area in cm².','- Volume = 3.0³ = **27 cm³**.\n- Volume = 27 × 10⁻⁶ = **2.7 × 10⁻⁵ m³**.\n- Surface area = 6 × 3.0² = **54 cm²**.','MS5b–5c;WS4.5'),
q(3,'A 400 N force acts normally on a 20 cm² surface. Calculate pressure in Pa.','- Area = 20 × 10⁻⁴ = **0.0020 m²**.\n- p = F/A = 400/0.0020.\n- **p = 2.0 × 10⁵ Pa**.','MS3c;MS5c'),
q(3,'An electromagnetic wave has wavelength 6.0 cm in vacuum. Calculate frequency using c = 3.0 × 10⁸ m/s.','- λ = **0.060 m**.\n- f = c/λ = (3.0 × 10⁸)/0.060.\n- **f = 5.0 × 10⁹ Hz**.','MS1b;MS3b–3c','2023 2H Q06.2: adapted conversion and substitution'),
q(3,'A 2.0 A current flows for 5.0 minutes through a 6.0 V component. Calculate charge and energy transferred.','- t = **300 s**, so Q = It = 2.0 × 300 = **600 C**.\n- E = QV = 600 × 6.0.\n- **E = 3,600 J**.','MS3c;Appendix A'),
q(3,'A 0.20 T field acts on a perpendicular 8.0 cm wire carrying 1.5 A. Calculate force.','- l = **0.080 m**.\n- F = BIl = 0.20 × 1.5 × 0.080.\n- **F = 0.024 N**.','MS3c;Appendix A','2022 2H Q07.5: related conversion'),
q(3,'The velocity–time area above zero is 30 m and the area below zero has magnitude 10 m. Calculate displacement and total distance.','- Signed displacement = 30 − 10 = **20 m** in the chosen positive direction.\n- Total distance = 30 + 10 = **40 m**.\n- Opposite-direction travel reduces displacement but still adds to distance.','MS4f'),
q(3,'A force of 600 N acts perpendicular to a lever, 0.050 m from the pivot. Calculate the moment and state why total lever length is not always used.','- M = Fd = 600 × 0.050 = **30 Nm**.\n- d is the **perpendicular distance to the force’s line of action**.\n- It need not equal the full length of the lever.','MS3c;MS5a'),
q(4,'An object accelerates uniformly from 3.0 m/s to 7.0 m/s over 10 m. Calculate acceleration using v² − u² = 2as.','- Rearrange: **a = (v² − u²)/(2s)**.\n- v² − u² = 7.0² − 3.0² = **40 m²/s²**.\n- a = 40/(2 × 10).\n- **a = 2.0 m/s²**, using constant acceleration.','MS3b–3d'),
q(4,'A 50 kg load is lifted 2.0 m in 5.0 s by a motor with efficiency 0.80. Use g = 10 N/kg. Calculate input power.','- Useful energy = mgh = 50 × 10 × 2.0 = **1,000 J**.\n- Useful power = 1,000/5.0 = **200 W**.\n- Input power = useful power/efficiency = 200/0.80.\n- **250 W**.','MS3b–3d;Appendix A'),
q(4,'Velocity rises linearly from 0 to 6.0 m/s in 4.0 s, then stays at 6.0 m/s for another 5.0 s. Calculate total displacement.','- Accelerating section: triangle area = ½ × 4.0 × 6.0 = **12 m**.\n- Steady section: rectangle area = 5.0 × 6.0 = **30 m**.\n- Total = 12 + 30 = **42 m**.\n- All velocities are positive, so distance also equals 42 m.','MS4f'),
q(4,'An ideal transformer has a 240 V primary with 1,200 turns and a secondary with 60 turns. Secondary current is 2.0 A. Calculate secondary voltage and primary current.','- Vₛ = 240 × 60/1,200 = **12 V**.\n- Secondary power = 12 × 2.0 = **24 W**.\n- Ideal primary power also equals 24 W.\n- Iₚ = 24/240 = **0.10 A**.','MS3b–3d;Appendix A','2022 2H Q07.2;2023 2H Q08.2: related linked calculations'),
q(4,'A student calculates a walking speed as 1.2 × 10⁴ m/s. Explain how to check and improve the calculation.','- Estimate expected walking speed as **order 1 m/s**, so the result is implausible.\n- Check distance and time conversions, especially **cm to m and minutes to seconds**.\n- Check that distance was divided by time rather than multiplied.\n- Recalculate with labelled quantities and units, keeping sensible significant figures.','MS1d;MS2h;MS3c'),
q(6,'A heater warms 0.50 kg of ice from −10°C to 0°C and then melts it completely. Use c_ice = 2,100 J/kg°C and L = 334,000 J/kg. Heater power is 500 W and 80% of its input reaches the ice. Calculate the required time.','- First stage is warming: ΔE₁ = mcΔθ = 0.50 × 2,100 × 10 = **10,500 J**.\n- Second stage is melting at constant temperature: E₂ = mL = 0.50 × 334,000 = **167,000 J**.\n- Total energy reaching the ice = **177,500 J**.\n- Efficiency = useful/input, so required input = 177,500/0.80 = **221,875 J**.\n- Time = E_input/P = 221,875/500 = **443.75 s**, about **440 s** to two significant figures.\n- The calculation separates warming from the state change and assumes constant power and the stated efficiency throughout.','MS3b–3d;Appendix A')]

